%I A399572 #26 Oct 05 2026 11:37:30
%S A399572 0,1,3,21,28,351,465,1081
%N A399572 Triangular numbers in the Padovan sequence A000931.
%C A399572 The sequence is finite: 8*A000931(k)+1 is an integer linear recurrence with a dominant root that is not the square of a linear recurrence on any arithmetic progression, so by a theorem of Corvaja and Zannier (2002) it is a square for only finitely many k.
%C A399572 Conjecture: the sequence is complete. No further terms: none of A000931(k) for 32 <= k <= 10^12 is triangular (8*A000931(k)+1 is a quadratic non-residue modulo at least one of the 40 smallest primes > 2^30, or, for k = 392532961617 and 972000569010, modulo 1000003 and 1000033 respectively). Any further term must have index k in one of 2365 residue classes modulo 55194480 (congruence obstructions from the primes 5, 7, 11, 19, 23, 89, 109, 271, 607, 691, 809, 829, 991, 1009, 3037, 3313, 3637). The corresponding indices k with A000931(k) triangular are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 17, 18, 27, 28, 31.
%C A399572 Compare the triangular Fibonacci numbers 1, 3, 21, 55 (Ming Luo, 1989) and triangular Lucas numbers 1, 3, 5778 (Ming Luo, 1991).
%H A399572 P. Corvaja and U. Zannier, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1015594913393">Some new applications of the subspace theorem</a>, Compositio Math. 131 (2002), 319-340.
%H A399572 Luo Ming, <a href="https://www.fq.math.ca/Scanned/27-2/ming.pdf">On Triangular Fibonacci Numbers</a>, The Fibonacci Quarterly, 27.2 (1989), pp. 98-108.
%H A399572 Luo Ming, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-011-3586-3_26">On Triangular Lucas Numbers</a>, Applications of Fibonacci Numbers, 1991, pp 231-240.
%e A399572 1081 = A000931(31) = 46*47/2 is a term.
%e A399572 465 = A000931(28) = 30*31/2 and 351 = A000931(27) = 26*27/2 are terms.
%p A399572 select(k-> issqr(8*k+1), {(<<0|1|0>, <0|0|1>, <1|1|0>>^n)[1, 1]$n=0..40})[];  # _Alois P. Heinz_, Oct 01 2026
%o A399572 (PARI) a=vector(200); a[1]=1; for(k=4, 200, a[k]=a[k-2]+a[k-3]); Set(select(v->ispolygonal(v, 3), a))
%o A399572 (Python)
%o A399572 from math import isqrt
%o A399572 a = [1, 0, 0]
%o A399572 while len(a) < 200:
%o A399572     a.append(a[-2] + a[-3])
%o A399572 print(sorted({v for v in a if isqrt(8*v+1)**2 == 8*v+1}))
%Y A399572 Intersection of A000217 and A000931.
%Y A399572 Cf. A039595, A248506.
%K A399572 nonn,fini,new
%O A399572 1,3
